WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.035
My name is Pedro Reyes

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.236
and I am an artist.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:04.604
I am the inaugural

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.239
Dasha Zhukova Distinguished

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:09.476
Artist in residency at MIT.

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.378
which is a residency for which

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:13.680
I made a project

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.648
called "Manufacturing Mischief"

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:16.783
which is a puppet show.

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:20.053
the play is part of

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:21.855
a project that I started

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.456
perhaps eleven years ago

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.457
working with puppets.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:27.060
The advantage of using puppets

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.863
to talk about philosophy

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.732
is that you don't need

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:35.168
to delve too much into

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:36.970
the personality aspects of

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.071
the philosopher,

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.072
but you can go more

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:40.040
into the ideas.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.141
[Chomsky] This is my point,

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:42.442
you cannot control the

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:43.710
mishaps of devices

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:44.844
that think for themselves.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:46.780
This is a very exciting

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.481
new development

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:50.850
because it features

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:52.485
Noam Chomsky as the protagonist

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:53.953
And it has to do with

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.756
certain questions about

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:58.825
the moral dimensions

00:00:58.892 --> 00:00:59.893
of technology.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:00.894
[Chomsky] The way forward

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:01.895
isn't an unchecked

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.129
approach to technology

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:04.631
But think of the good we

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:05.999
could do professor

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.333
Some of the fears that one

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.335
may have about

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:11.037
the destruction of labor

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:12.105
due to automation,

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.473
or that often

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.408
technology eccelerates

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.277
the inequity gap,

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:18.445
or the blocks to

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:19.813
democracy because

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:23.083
of artificial intellegence, etc,

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:25.185
have been addressed

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:28.154
by Professor Chomsky

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.824
so it was a big

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.928
inspiration to use

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:37.497
some of his writings

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:39.332
to develop a play.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.901
[Chomsky] Zyklon B

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.437
Fritz Haber

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:45.538
Polish Jew

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.307
won the Nobel Peace Prize

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:48.374
for creating cheap

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:49.442
nitrogen fertilizer.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:50.577
[Student] See? I want to

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:51.644
help like that!

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:52.812
[Chomsky] And when the

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:53.813
Nazis got ahold of

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:54.814
his altruistic chemical

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:55.815
they pumped it into

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:56.816
the gas chambers

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:57.951
and killed

00:01:58.084 --> 00:01:59.285
1.2 million people.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:01.921
It is important

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:02.922
to take into

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:03.923
consideration that

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:04.924
technology doesn't have

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:05.925
a moral value

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:06.926
in itself.

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.128
It can be used

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:09.362
both for good

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:10.630
and for evil.

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:21.174
[♪]

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:24.010
The play is

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.545
a comedy.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:26.679
Which is a format

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:27.780
that allows

00:02:27.847 --> 00:02:28.848
us to address

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:29.949
all these

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:30.984
subjects in a

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.252
very lighthearted manor.

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:33.253
Puppets allow you

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:34.254
to break a lot of rules

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:35.455
It's a very forgiving medium

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:36.689
in the sense that

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.124
you can make

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.592
very silly encounters

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:41.394
In the case of

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.096
Manufacturing Mischief,

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:44.497
you have persons

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:45.698
from different moments

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:46.699
in history

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:47.700
that never had

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.701
the chance to

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:49.702
meet in real life

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:50.703
But they're coming

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:51.738
together, and

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.139
what is bringing

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:54.474
them together is

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:56.009
the Deus ex Machina

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:57.177
which is a machine

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.478
called the
Print-a-Friend.

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:03.216
You put a book

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:04.217
into the

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:05.218
Print-a-Friend

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:06.219
and out comes

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.220
the author.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.221
It's like bringing

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:09.622
the author to life

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.524
No explanations are

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:13.092
given on how

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:14.594
this happens.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:15.595
[Chomsky] Are you...?

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:16.596
Karl Marx?

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:17.597
[Student] Surprise!

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.165
All of these

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:20.266
characters start

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:21.968
to come back.

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.403
Karl Marx is one of them

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:24.637
Ayn Rand is another.

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:25.905
And even Trump

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:27.307
himself emerges

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:28.608
out of the

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:30.076
Print-a-Friend.

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.379
The play is

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:34.747
trying to remind

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:36.049
us about

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:37.417
the importance

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.118
for a human dimension

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.720
when it comes to

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.188
technology.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.357
How important it is

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:46.492
to keep in mind

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:49.028
what are the social costs

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.531
that embracing a

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:53.499
technology will have.

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:04.611
[Student] Oh my god

